{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and he inherited his title after his father was assassinated by the Order of Assassins. His early reign involved securing the borders of his county against Muslim forces while navigating complex political alliances within the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond spent time as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din which shaped his diplomatic approach and his understanding of Islamic leadership styles. He later became a key advisor to King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and needed strong nobles to support his rule.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond assumed power at a young age following the violent death of his father and he immediately faced threats from neighboring Muslim emirs. The young count managed to stabilize his realm through careful negotiation rather than constant warfare. His captivity provided him with unique insights into the culture and strategies of his enemies and this knowledge proved valuable throughout his career. He learned to speak Arabic fluently and developed relationships with Muslim leaders that few other Crusader nobles could match.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count served as regent for Baldwin IV during periods when the king was too ill to govern alone. Raymond advocated for peaceful coexistence with Saladin and he opposed aggressive military campaigns that he believed would weaken the Christian kingdoms. His faction clashed with the court party led by Guy of Lusignan and Raynald of Châtillon who favored confrontation. These internal divisions weakened the kingdom and made it vulnerable to external attacks. Raymond tried to prevent the disastrous Battle of Hattin by advising caution but his counsel was ignored by the reckless leadership of Guy.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nRaymond withdrew to Tripoli after the crushing defeat at Hattin and he died shortly thereafter in 1187. Historians debate whether his death resulted from natural causes or illness brought on by the stress of political failure. His legacy remains complicated because he was seen as a pragmatist by some and a traitor by others due to his willingness to negotiate with Muslims. He left no direct heir so the county passed to a distant relative. His life illustrates the fragile nature of the Crusader states and the difficult choices leaders faced when survival depended on diplomacy as much as swordplay. The loss of his strategic vision contributed significantly to the fall of Jerusalem later that same year.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.001363e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00037844188}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00032585207}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0095856385}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.6584024e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0072059357}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.014714503}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.013375345}, {"token": " role", "logpro